A||||8||mbramble||Shocks/Struts||||1||
Z||000000||mbramble||07-27-2000||09:55 PM||||OK, it's about time to replace them on my 87 2.5L Coupe.  I just want to replace them with OEM type equipment, nothing fancy on this daily driver.<P>What do I need to buy?? And are there any tricks, tips, problems in doing it yourself??  If it was my pickup truck, I'd go buy 4 shocks and replace them on Saturday.  This 'strut' stuff is foreign to me and I would like some idea as to what I'd be getting in to...  Seems like I would need to borrow a spring compressor for doing the rear struts??<P>Any help, advice, etc., would be most appreciated.<P>Thanks in advance,<BR>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||2birds||07-27-2000||10:15 PM||||Front shocks are a piece of cake, all you need are a 13 and 2 15 mm sockets/wrenches and pulling the wheel to make access easier.  <BR>The struts are a whole different ball game.  If you don't have access to air tools and a strut spring compressor, it's a real PITA to remove and swap the springs to the new ones.  Most (but not all) shops will charge you a reasonable fee to do the swap for you.  The real problem is getting the strut to bearing carrier bolts out (22 or 24 mm socket, I believe) and a breaker bar for removal, and a torque wrench for re-install.  Then there's that little matter of your alignment being all whacked out in back, possibly making the car undrivable.  <BR>Bottom line is, the struts are best left to a shop, unless you already have the tools and patience, and are good at eyeballing the rear alignment (or own your own tow truck).<BR>As for brands, Monroe Gasmatics have served me well for 10 years, and are reasonably priced, but if you lower it I think something better would be needed.  I had Gabriel Red Ryders on the front for about a year, until they started leaking fluid.  KYB's are also reasonable, but I've never tried them on a Fiero.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||2FAST4U||07-27-2000||10:25 PM||||Front shocks go on just like on your pickup. For rear struts you will need a pair of spring compressors. The kind that fit on the outside of the sping. Loosen the lug neuts. Jack the rear of the car and put it on jack stands. You'll need the jack later. remove the rear tires. Scribe a mark around area where the strut bolts to the knuckle. Put the jack under the a-arm and jack it up just enough to take up slack (not enough to compress the spring). Remove the three retaining nuts on the top of the fender well in the engine compartment. Lower the jack completely. Now unbolt the strut assembly from the knukle and remove the whole assembly from the car. Repeat for the other side. Compress the springs with the spring compressors ( one on each side of the spring. Remove the retaining nut on the top of the strut assembly. Take note of how the top retaining plate and associated parts go together. reverse procedure using the scribe marks to reattach the strut to the knuckle to reassemble. You'll have to have an alignment after changing the struts. I use Monroe gas shocks and struts. Good quality and fair price. Depending on where you buy your stuff you may be able to get them to change out your strut cartridges for little or no charge. If so, then you don't need the spring compressors.<P>Hope this helps<P>Joe<BR>(2fast4u)<p>[This message has been edited by 2FAST4U (edited 07-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||07-28-2000||02:40 AM||||For changing struts yourself, I just brought my struts with the new ones to a shop and for $30.00 they pulled apart the old and installed the new. If you do the struts I would also suggest buying the "strut cam bolt" kit for them. Basically the bottom bolts have an offset washer to aid in ajusting the rear camber. The alignment shops can get the camber just about perfect.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||07-28-2000||12:34 PM||||Yep, what the other guys said. I wanted to add that many big auto parts chain stores have a free tool loaner program. You put a deposit down and if you return the spring compressor in good condition and within the alloted time (2 days for most I've borrowed), then you get your money back.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 07-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||07-29-2000||01:41 AM||||Autozone will return your $$ no matter how long you've had the tool.  I just returned a steering wheel puller after about 2 1/2 months.  I got my deposit back.||||reg||1
Z||000006||WKDFIRO||07-30-2000||05:41 AM||||In my experience, I have never had to use spring compressors to change the struts. Most if not all of the tension is off when the strut is taken out of the car. The nut keeps it from "springing" into your face and and a little push down is all you need to take the nut off. Putting the spring back on just requires the strut on the ground me standing over it and pushing down on the spring a little to start the nut. This is with the GT WS6 springs (I forget the actual part number for the spring) so if you are stalling because of spring compressors don't, because thats what I did. I felt kinda foolish when I didn't use them. <P>Now Im not saying its foolish to be safe! Im just saying that we never needed to use them at Fiero Motorsports. Lowering coils were even easier!<P>Hope this helps.<BR> ||||reg||5
Z||000007||batboy||07-30-2000||08:11 AM||||I dunno, there's still a chance of it going ballistic. One thing you can do is chain the spring down BEFORE you jack it up, while it's still compressed. Then you don't have to use a spring compressor. Leave the spring chained until it's securely installed on the new strut and back on the car. Then lower the jack and take the nut and bolt off that holds the chain together.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mbramble||07-30-2000||11:12 AM||||Thanks for the info guys.  Guess I need to go and take a look under the rear of the car to see exactly what you are all talking about and then make a descision as to whether to try myself or take into the shop.||||reg||1
